diff --git a/docs/web/generic-parallel.md b/docs/web/generic-parallel.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7e76d6b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/web/generic-parallel.md
@@ -0,0 +1,289 @@
+---
+title: Batches of integration points (`generic-parallel` interface)
+date: 2026
+lang: en-EN
+numbersections: true
+documentclass: article
+from: markdown+tex_math_single_backslash
+geometry:
+  - margin=2cm
+papersize: a4
+link-citations: true
+colorlinks: true
+figPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+tabPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+secPrefixTemplate: "$$i$$"
+eqnPrefixTemplate: "($$i$$)"
+---
+
+The `generic-parallel` interface of `MFront` generates, for a behaviour, a
+model or a material property, functions processing a whole batch of
+points in one call. The same `MFront` file can be compiled for several
+backends: `CUDA`, `HIP`, the parallel algorithms of the `C++` standard
+library, `Kokkos`, `SYCL` and `OpenMP`. A generated library targets one
+backend, and may compute on the host or on a GPU. The interface is
+described in the documentation of `MFront`.
+
+`MGIS` recognizes those libraries when they are loaded and uses them
+through its usual objects (`Behaviour`, `MaterialDataManager`,
+`MaterialProperty`). This page describes how.
+
+# Overview
+
+- The existing functions of `MGIS` acting on a material data manager
+  (`integrate`, `executeInitializeFunction`, `executePostProcessing`, the
+  rotations) automatically switch to the batch functions of a
+  `generic-parallel` library. The calling code is unchanged.
+- Each batch function also exists with an execution policy, which selects
+  the queue of the computations (`CUDA` stream, `SYCL` queue, etc.).
+- No data is copied: the batch is described by views on the arrays of the
+  material data manager.
+- The calling code declares where its arrays are located. `MGIS` checks
+  that the computations of the library can access them.
+- `MGIS` does not depend on any GPU toolkit. The arrays are given by the
+  calling code, or allocated by a memory resource given by the calling
+  code.
+
+# Loading
+
+The `load` functions read the symbols describing a library. For a library
+generated by the `generic-parallel` interface, the version of its data
+structures is checked, and the backend and the memory space of the library
+are read. They are stored in the description of the behaviour (members
+`interface`, `backend` and `memory_space`). The
+`isGenericParallelBehaviour` function tells if a behaviour processes
+batches.
+
+`MGIS` has its own copies of the data structures of `TFEL`
+(`MGIS/Behaviour/BatchDataView.hxx`), as for the `generic` interface: it
+does not depend on `TFEL` at compile time.
+
+The functions acting on a single integration point (`integrate` on a
+`BehaviourDataView`, `integrate_debug`, the initialize functions, the
+post-processings and the rotations of a point, the `C` bindings) report an
+error for a `generic-parallel` library. Conversely, the batch functions
+report an error for a library of the `generic` interface.
+
+# Integration of a batch
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+auto r = integrate(policy, m, opts, dt);        // all the points
+auto r = integrate(policy, m, opts, dt, b, e);  // points [b, e)
+~~~~
+
+The overloads without execution policy use the policy of the material
+data manager (`batch_execution_policy` member). The overloads taking a
+thread pool make a single call: the parallelism is the one of the
+backend.
+
+The data of the material data manager are mapped as follows:
+
+- the gradients, the thermodynamic forces, the internal state variables
+  and the energies are fields stored point by point;
+- the material properties and the external state variables are given
+  variable by variable: each one is uniform or given point by point, as in
+  the `MaterialStateManager`. The elements of an array of variables are
+  distinct variables;
+- the mass density is only given when the speed of sound is computed.
+
+Contrary to the integration point by point, all the points of the batch
+are integrated, even if the integration fails at some points. The result
+gives the index of the first point where the integration failed
+(member `n`, an absolute index) and the number of failures in the error
+message. The detailed error message of a point can be obtained by
+integrating this point with the library of the `generic` interface of the
+same behaviour (see [this page](behaviour-integration-failure-analysis.html)).
+
+# Initialize functions, post-processings and rotations
+
+They follow the same pattern:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+executeInitializeFunction(policy, m, name[, inputs][, b, e]);
+executePostProcessing(outputs, policy, m, name[, b, e]);
+rotateGradients(policy, destination, behaviour, source, r);
+~~~~
+
+The overloads without execution policy switch automatically. The
+rotation matrices can be uniform or given point by point. The
+`RotationMatrix2D` and `RotationMatrix3D` objects build the rotation
+matrices on the host at each call: for repeated rotations on a GPU, the
+matrices shall rather be built once and given as an array.
+
+# Models
+
+The models generated by the `generic-parallel` interface export the same
+functions and symbols as the behaviours. `mgis::model::load` loads them as
+behaviours: they are integrated by the same batch functions, and their
+parameters are set by `setParameter`.
+
+# Material properties
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+const auto mp = mgis::material_property::load(library, "YoungModulus");
+const auto inputs = std::array{std::span<const mgis::real>(temperatures)};
+const auto s1 = evaluate(values, mp, inputs);
+const auto s2 = evaluate(policy, values, mp, inputs, MemoryLocation::SHARED);
+~~~~
+
+When a material property generated by the `generic-parallel` interface is
+loaded, `MGIS` reads the version of its functions, its backend and its
+memory space, and loads the function evaluating a set of points
+(`batch_fct` member) instead of the function evaluating one point (`fct`
+member). `isGenericParallelMaterialProperty` tells if a material property
+evaluates sets of points.
+
+The `evaluate` function evaluates a material property on a set of points.
+Each input is uniform (one value) or given point by point. As
+`integrate`, it makes one call for a `generic-parallel` material property,
+and evaluates the other material properties point by point on the host.
+The returned status follows the conventions of the `generic` interface of
+the material properties. For a `generic-parallel` material property, it
+summarizes all the points: no index of point is reported.
+
+# Memory
+
+## Location of the arrays and memory space of a library
+
+The calling code declares the location of its arrays
+(`mgis::MemoryLocation`):
+
+- `HOST`: ordinary memory of the host;
+- `DEVICE`: memory of a device, not accessible by the host;
+- `SHARED`: memory accessible by the host and the device (managed memory
+  of `CUDA` or `HIP`, shared memory of `SYCL`, `Kokkos::SharedSpace`).
+
+The location of the arrays of a material data manager is given by its
+initializer (`location` member of `MaterialDataManagerInitializer`). The
+location of the values of a material property is an argument of
+`evaluate`.
+
+`MGIS` compares this location to the memory space exported by the
+library (`isMemoryLocationAccessible`):
+
+| Memory space of the library | `HOST` | `DEVICE` | `SHARED` |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `host` (and libraries of the `generic` interface) | yes | no | yes |
+| `device` | no | yes | yes |
+| `any` | yes | yes | yes |
+
+`MGIS` thus knows nothing about the backends: a new backend of `TFEL`
+requires no change in `MGIS`.
+
+## Functions working on the host
+
+Some functions of `MGIS` read the arrays on the host: `update`, `revert`,
+`updateValues`, `extractInternalStateVariable`, `save`, `restore` and the
+integration point by point. They report an error if the arrays are located
+on a device. `allocatePostProcessingVariables` allocates the outputs of a
+post-processing on the host: if the behaviour can't access them, they
+must be allocated by the calling code.
+
+The values held by `MGIS` (uniform values and values stored with the
+`LOCAL_STORAGE` mode) are stored on the host. They are refused when the
+computations can't access the memory of the host: they must be given by a
+`std::span` on accessible memory, with the `EXTERNAL_STORAGE` mode. A
+uniform value can be given by a `std::span` of one value.
+
+## Memory resources
+
+A material data manager allocates the arrays that its initializer does not
+give. By default, those arrays are allocated on the host. The initializer
+can give a memory resource (`resource` member, a
+`std::pmr::memory_resource*`), used to allocate them. `MGIS` initializes
+those arrays on the host: the resource must allocate memory accessible by
+the host, in practice shared memory. If the arrays are located on a
+device, all of them must be given by the initializer.
+
+Four memory resources are provided as headers, compiled by the calling
+code with its own GPU toolkit (`MGIS/MemoryResources/`):
+
+| Header | Memory |
+|---|---|
+| `CUDAManagedMemoryResource.hxx` | managed memory of `CUDA` |
+| `HIPManagedMemoryResource.hxx` | managed memory of `HIP` |
+| `SYCLSharedMemoryResource.hxx` | shared memory of `SYCL`, for a given queue |
+| `KokkosSharedSpaceMemoryResource.hxx` | `Kokkos::SharedSpace` |
+
+# Execution policy
+
+`mgis::BatchExecutionPolicy` is shared by the behaviours, the models and
+the material properties:
+
+- `queue`: queue of the computations, whose type depends on the backend
+  (`cudaStream_t`, `hipStream_t`, pointer to a `sycl::queue` or to an
+  instance of a `Kokkos` execution space). A null pointer denotes the
+  default queue, if meaningful for the backend (a queue is required by the
+  `SYCL` backend).
+- `preserve_stack_size`: if true, the stack size of the threads of the
+  device is never modified, and an error is reported if it is too small.
+  Otherwise, the stack size is increased if required. This affects the
+  whole process, since the stack size is a property of the context of the
+  device (`CUDA` and `HIP`).
+
+The overloads without execution policy use the policy of the material data
+manager, or a default policy for the rotations and the material
+properties. The default policy gives a null queue, which the `SYCL`
+backend refuses.
+
+The computations performed by `MGIS` are synchronous: the functions return
+once the computations are completed.
+
+# Errors
+
+`MGIS` distinguishes two cases:
+
+- a failure of the computations at some points (lack of convergence,
+  physical bound violated) is a result: the status is `-1`, and the index
+  of the first point where the computations failed is reported;
+- invalid arguments and errors of the backend are reported by exceptions.
+  They are never reported as a failure of the computations.
+
+For the material properties, the statuses `-5`, `-6` and `-7` (invalid
+arguments, runtime error of the backend, invalid strides) are reported
+by exceptions. As with the `generic` interface, the status `-2` (exception
+thrown on the host by the generated function) is returned.
+
+The batch functions report errors by exceptions, as the other functions
+acting on a material data manager. They have no variant taking an
+execution context.
+
+# Current limitations
+
+- The asynchronous computations offered by `TFEL` are not exposed.
+- The optional arrays giving the status and the time step scaling factor
+  of each point are not exposed.
+- The `C`, `Fortran` and `Python` bindings do not expose the new
+  functions. The `C` and `Fortran` functions acting on a single point
+  report a failure for a `generic-parallel` library.
+
+# Example
+
+A behaviour compiled for `CUDA`, the arrays of the material data manager
+allocated by `MGIS` in managed memory, the computations submitted to a
+stream chosen by the calling code. The temperature is given by a
+`std::span` of one value located in managed memory.
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/Behaviour.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/MaterialDataManager.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/Behaviour/Integrate.hxx"
+#include "MGIS/MemoryResources/CUDAManagedMemoryResource.hxx"
+
+using namespace mgis::behaviour;
+// the resource must outlive the material data manager
+auto resource = mgis::memory_resources::CUDAManagedMemoryResource{};
+const auto b =
+    load("libBehaviour-cuda.so", "Norton", Hypothesis::TRIDIMENSIONAL);
+auto i = MaterialDataManagerInitializer{};
+i.location = MemoryLocation::SHARED;
+i.resource = &resource;
+auto m = MaterialDataManager(b, n, i);
+for (auto* const s : {&m.s0, &m.s1}) {
+  setExternalStateVariable(*s, "Temperature", temperature,
+                           MaterialStateManager::EXTERNAL_STORAGE);
+}
+auto policy = BatchExecutionPolicy{};
+policy.queue = stream;
+const auto r = integrate(policy, m, BehaviourIntegrationOptions{}, dt);
+~~~~
